## sn42.11
sn42.11:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Mallas, near the Mallian town named Uruvelakappa. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Mallans in a Mallan town named Uruvelakappa.
sn42.11:1.2 | S: Then Bhadraka the village chief went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Gandhabhaka the headman went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
sn42.11:1.3 | S: “Please, sir, let the Blessed One teach me the origin and disappearance of suffering.” | T: “It would be good, lord, if the Blessed One would teach me the origination & ending of stress.”
sn42.11:1.4 | S: “Chief, if I were to teach you about the origin and disappearance of suffering in connection with the past, saying | T: “Headman, if I were to teach you the origination & ending of stress with reference to the past, saying,
sn42.11:1.5 | S: ‘this is how it was in the past,’ you might have doubts or uncertainties about that. | T: ‘Thus it was in the past,’ you would be doubtful and perplexed.
sn42.11:1.6 | S: If I were to teach you about the origin and disappearance of suffering in connection with the future, saying | T: If I were to teach you the origination & ending of stress with reference to the future, saying,
sn42.11:1.7 | S: ‘this is how it will be in the future,’ you might have doubts or uncertainties about that. | T: ‘Thus it will be in the future,’ you would be doubtful and perplexed.
sn42.11:1.8 | S: Rather, chief, I will teach you about the origin and disappearance of suffering as I am sitting right here and you are sitting right there. | T: So instead, I—sitting right here—will teach you sitting right there the origination & ending of stress.
sn42.11:1.9 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
sn42.11:1.10 | S: “Yes, sir,” Bhadraka replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” Gandhabhaka the headman responded to him.
sn42.11:1.11 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
sn42.11:2.1 | S: “What do you think, chief? | T: “Now what do you think, headman?
sn42.11:2.2 | S: Are there any people here in Uruvelakappa who, if they were executed, imprisoned, fined, or condemned, it would cause you sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress?” | T: Are there any people in Uruvelakappa who, if they were murdered or imprisoned or fined or censured, would cause sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, or despair to arise in you?”
sn42.11:2.3 | S: “There are, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord, there are people in Uruvelakappa who, if they were murdered or imprisoned or fined or censured, would cause sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, or despair to arise in me.”
sn42.11:2.4 | S: “But are there any people here in Uruvelakappa who, if they were executed, imprisoned, fined, or condemned, it would not cause you sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress?” | T: “And are there any people in Uruvelakappa who, if they were murdered or imprisoned or fined or censured, would cause no sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, or despair to arise in you?”
sn42.11:2.5 | S: “There are, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord, there are people in Uruvelakappa who, if they were murdered or imprisoned or fined or censured, would cause no sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, or despair to arise in me.”
sn42.11:2.6 | S: “What’s the cause, chief, what’s the reason why, if this was to happen to some people it could cause you sorrow, while if it happens to others it does not?” | T: “Now what is the cause, what is the reason, why the murder, imprisonment, fining, or censure of some of the people in Uruvelakappa would cause you sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, or despair, whereas the murder, imprisonment, fining, or censure of others would cause you no sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, or despair?“
sn42.11:2.7 | S: “The people regarding whom this would give rise to sorrow are those I have desire and passion. | T: “Those people in Uruvelakappa whose murder, imprisonment, fining, or censure would cause me sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, or despair are those for whom I feel desire-passion.
sn42.11:2.8 | S: The people regarding whom this would not give rise to sorrow are those I don’t have desire and passion.” | T: Those people in Uruvelakappa whose murder, imprisonment, fining, or censure would cause me no sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, or despair are those for whom I feel no desire-passion.”
sn42.11:2.9 | S: “With this present phenomenon that you have seen, understood, immediately attained, and fathomed, you can apply the same method to the past and future: | T: “Now, headman, from what you have realized, fathomed, attained right now in the present, without regard to time, you may draw an inference with regard to the past and future:
sn42.11:2.10 | S: ‘All the suffering that arose in the past was rooted and sourced in desire. | T: ‘Whatever stress, in arising, arose for me in the past, all of it had desire as its root, had desire as its cause—
sn42.11:2.11 | S: For desire is the root of suffering. | T: for desire is the cause of stress.
sn42.11:2.12 | S: All the suffering that will arise in the future will be rooted and sourced in desire. | T: And whatever stress, in arising, will arise for me in the future, all of it will have desire as the root, will have desire as its cause—
sn42.11:2.13 | S: For desire is the root of suffering.’” | T: for desire is the cause of stress.’”
sn42.11:2.14 | S: “It’s incredible, sir, it’s amazing! | T: “Amazing, lord! Astounding!
sn42.11:2.15 | S: How well said this was by the Buddha! | T: How well the Blessed One has put it:
sn42.11:2.16 | S: ‘All the suffering that arises is rooted and sourced in desire. | T: ‘Whatever stress, in arising, arose for me in the past, all of it had desire as its root, had desire as its cause—for desire is the cause of stress. And whatever stress, in arising, will arise for me in the future, all of it will have desire as the root, will have desire as its cause—
sn42.11:2.17 | S: For desire is the root of suffering.’ | T: for desire is the cause of stress.’
sn42.11:2.18 | S: I have a boy called Ciravāsi, who resides in a house away from here. | T: I have a son, lord, named Ciravāsi, who lives far away from here.
sn42.11:2.19 | S: I rise early and dispatch someone, saying: | T: When I get up in the morning, I send a man, saying,
sn42.11:2.20 | S: ‘Go, lad, and check on my boy Ciravāsi.’ | T: ‘Go, learn how Ciravāsi is doing.’
sn42.11:2.21 | S: Until they get back I worry: | T: And as long as that man has not returned, I am simply beside myself, (thinking,)
sn42.11:2.22 | S: ‘I hope nothing’s wrong with Ciravāsi!’” | T: ‘Don’t let Ciravāsi be sick!’”
sn42.11:3.1 | S: “What do you think, chief? | T: “Now, what do you think, headman?
sn42.11:3.2 | S: If Ciravāsi was executed, imprisoned, fined, or condemned, would it cause you sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress?” | T: If Ciravāsi were to be murdered or imprisoned or fined or censured, would you feel sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair?”
sn42.11:3.3 | S: “How could it not, sir?” | T: “Lord, if my son Ciravāsi were to be murdered or imprisoned or fined or censured, my very life would be altered. So how could I not feel sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair?”
