an3.61:2.3 | S: ‘Is it really true that this is the venerables’ view?’ | T: (none)
an3.61:2.4 | P: “yaṁ kiñcāyaṁ purisapuggalo paṭisaṁvedeti sukhaṁ vā dukkhaṁ vā adukkhamasukhaṁ vā sabbaṁ taṁ pubbekatahetū”’ti? | T: (none)
an3.61:2.5 | S: And they answered, ‘Yes’. | T: Thus asked by me, they admitted, ‘Yes.’
an3.61:2.6 | S: I said to them: | T: Then I said to them,
an3.61:2.7 | S: ‘In that case, you might kill living creatures, steal, be unchaste; use speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical; be covetous, malicious, or have wrong view, all because of past deeds.’ | T: ‘Then in that case, a person is a killer of living beings because of what was done in the past. A person is a thief… uncelibate… a liar… a divisive speaker… a harsh speaker… an idle chatterer… greedy… malicious… a holder of wrong views because of what was done in the past.’
an3.61:3.1 | S: Those who believe that past deeds are the most important thing have no enthusiasm or effort, no idea that there are things that should and should not be done. | T: When one falls back on what was done in the past as being essential, monks, there is no desire, no effort (at the thought), ‘This should be done. This shouldn’t be done.’
an3.61:3.2 | S: Not finding as a genuine fact that there are things that should and should not be done, they’re unmindful and careless, and can’t rightly be called ascetics. | T: When one can’t pin down as a truth or reality what should & shouldn’t be done, one dwells bewildered & unprotected. One cannot righteously refer to oneself as a contemplative.
an3.61:3.3 | S: This is my first legitimate refutation of the ascetics and brahmins who have this doctrine and view. | T: This was my first righteous refutation of those contemplatives & brahmans who hold to such teachings, such views.
an3.61:4.1 | S: Regarding this, I went up to the ascetics and brahmins whose view is that | T: “Having approached the contemplatives & brahmans who hold that…
an3.61:4.2 | S: everything that an individual person experiences is because of God Almighty’s creation, and I said to them: | T: ‘Whatever a person experiences… is all caused by a supreme being’s act of creation,’ I said to them: ‘Is it true that you hold that… whatever a person experiences… is all caused by a supreme being’s act of creation?’
an3.61:4.3 | S: ‘Is it really true that this is the venerables’ view?’ | T: (none)
an3.61:4.4 | P: yaṁ kiñcāyaṁ purisapuggalo paṭisaṁvedeti sukhaṁ vā dukkhaṁ vā adukkhamasukhaṁ vā sabbaṁ taṁ issaranimmānahetū’ti? | T: (none)
an3.61:4.5 | S: And they answered, ‘Yes’. | T: Thus asked by me, they admitted, ‘Yes.’
an3.61:4.6 | S: I said to them: | T: Then I said to them,
an3.61:4.7 | S: ‘In that case, you might kill living creatures, steal, be unchaste; use speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical; be covetous, malicious, or have wrong view, all because of God Almighty’s creation.’ | T: ‘Then in that case, a person is a killer of living beings because of a supreme being’s act of creation. A person is a thief… uncelibate… a liar… a divisive speaker… a harsh speaker… an idle chatterer… greedy… malicious… a holder of wrong views because of a supreme being’s act of creation.’
an3.61:5.1 | S: Those who believe that God Almighty’s creative power is the most important thing have no enthusiasm, no effort, no idea that there are things that should and should not be done. | T: When one falls back on a supreme being’s act of creation as being essential, monks, there is no desire, no effort (at the thought), ‘This should be done. This shouldn’t be done.’
an3.61:5.2 | S: Not finding as a genuine fact that there are things that should and should not be done, they’re unmindful and careless, and can’t rightly be called ascetics. | T: When one can’t pin down as a truth or reality what should & shouldn’t be done, one dwells bewildered & unprotected. One cannot righteously refer to oneself as a contemplative.
an3.61:5.3 | S: This is my second legitimate refutation of the ascetics and brahmins who have this doctrine and view. | T: This was my second righteous refutation of those contemplatives & brahmans who hold to such teachings, such views.
an3.61:6.1 | S: Regarding this, I went up to the ascetics and brahmins whose view is that | T: “Having approached the contemplatives & brahmans who hold that…
an3.61:6.2 | S: everything that an individual person experiences has no cause or reason, and I said to them: | T: ‘Whatever a person experiences… is all without cause, without condition,’ I said to them:
an3.61:6.3 | S: ‘Is it really true that this is the venerables’ view?’ | T: ‘Is it true that you hold that…
an3.61:6.4 | P: yaṁ kiñcāyaṁ purisapuggalo paṭisaṁvedeti sukhaṁ vā dukkhaṁ vā adukkhamasukhaṁ vā sabbaṁ taṁ ahetuappaccayā’ti? | T: whatever a person experiences… is all without cause, without condition?’
an3.61:6.5 | S: And they answered, ‘Yes’. | T: Thus asked by me, they admitted, ‘Yes.’
an3.61:6.6 | S: I said to them: | T: Then I said to them,
an3.61:6.7 | S: ‘In that case, you might kill living creatures, steal, be unchaste; use speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical; be covetous, malicious, or have wrong view, all without cause or reason.’ | T: ‘Then in that case, a person is a killer of living beings without cause, without condition. A person is a thief… uncelibate… a liar… a divisive speaker… a harsh speaker… an idle chatterer… greedy… malicious… a holder of wrong views without cause, without condition.’
an3.61:7.1 | S: Those who believe that the absence of cause or reason is the most important thing have no enthusiasm, no effort, no idea that there are things that should and should not be done. | T: When one falls back on lack of cause and lack of condition as being essential, monks, there is no desire, no effort (at the thought), ‘This should be done. This shouldn’t be done.’
an3.61:7.2 | S: Not finding as a genuine fact that there are things that should and should not be done, they’re unmindful and careless, and can’t rightly be called ascetics. | T: When one can’t pin down as a truth or reality what should & shouldn’t be done, one dwells bewildered & unprotected. One cannot righteously refer to oneself as a contemplative.
an3.61:7.3 | S: This is my third legitimate refutation of the ascetics and brahmins who have this doctrine and view. | T: This was my third righteous refutation of those contemplatives & brahmans who hold to such teachings, such views.
an3.61:8.1 | S: These are the three sectarian tenets—as pursued, pressed, and grilled by the astute—which, when taken to their conclusion, end with inaction. | T: “These are the three sectarian guilds that—when cross-examined, pressed for reasons, & rebuked by wise people—even though they may explain otherwise, remain stuck in inaction.
an3.61:9.1 | S: But the Dhamma that I’ve taught is irrefutable, uncorrupted, beyond reproach, and not scorned by sensible ascetics and brahmins. | T: “But this Dhamma taught by me is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans.
an3.61:9.2 | S: What is the Dhamma that I’ve taught? | T: And which Dhamma taught by me is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans?
an3.61:9.3 | S: ‘These are the six elements’: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught … | T: ‘There are these six properties’ is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans.
an3.61:9.4 | S: ‘These are the six fields of contact’: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught … | T: ‘There are these six media of sensory contact’ is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans.
an3.61:9.5 | S: ‘These are the eighteen mental preoccupations’: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught … | T: ‘There are these eighteen explorations for the intellect’ is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans.
an3.61:9.6 | S: ‘These are the four noble truths’: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught that is irrefutable, uncorrupted, beyond reproach, and is not scorned by sensible ascetics and brahmins. | T: ‘There are these four noble truths’ is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans.
an3.61:10.1 | S: ‘“These are the six elements”: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught …’ | T: “‘“There are these six properties” is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans’:
an3.61:10.2 | S: That’s what I said, | T: Thus was it said.
an3.61:10.3 | S: but why did I say it? | T: And in reference to what was it said?
an3.61:10.4 | S: There are these six elements: | T: These are the six properties:
an3.61:10.5 | S: the elements of earth, water, fire, air, space, and consciousness. | T: earth-property, liquid-property, fire-property, wind-property, space-property, consciousness-property.
an3.61:10.6 | S: ‘“These are the six elements”: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught …’ | T: ‘“There are these six properties” is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans’:
an3.61:10.7 | S: That’s what I said, and this is why I said it. | T: Thus was it said. And in reference to this was it said.
an3.61:11.1 | S: ‘“These are the six fields of contact”: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught …’ | T: “‘“There are these six media of sensory contact” is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans’:
an3.61:11.2 | S: That’s what I said, | T: Thus was it said.
an3.61:11.3 | S: but why did I say it? | T: And in reference to what was it said?
an3.61:11.4 | S: There are these six fields of contact: | T: These are the six media of sensory contact:
an3.61:11.5 | S: eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind contact. | T: the eye as a medium of sensory contact, the ear as a medium of sensory contact, the nose as a medium of sensory contact, the tongue as a medium of sensory contact, the body as a medium of sensory contact, the intellect as a medium of sensory contact.
an3.61:11.6 | S: ‘“These are the six fields of contact”: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught …’ | T: ‘“There are these six media of sensory contact” is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans’:
an3.61:11.7 | S: That’s what I said, and this is why I said it. | T: Thus was it said. And in reference to this was it said.
an3.61:12.1 | S: ‘“These are the eighteen mental preoccupations”: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught …’ | T: “‘“There are these eighteen explorations for the intellect” is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans’:
an3.61:12.2 | S: This is what I said, | T: Thus was it said.
an3.61:12.3 | S: but why did I say it? | T: And in reference to what was it said?
an3.61:12.4 | S: Seeing a sight with their eyes, one is preoccupied with a sight that’s a basis for happiness or sadness or equanimity. | T: Seeing a form via the eye, one explores a form that can act as the basis for happiness, one explores a form that can act as the basis for unhappiness, one explores a form that can act as the basis for equanimity.
an3.61:12.5 | S: Hearing a sound with their ears … | T: Hearing a sound via the ear…
an3.61:12.6 | S: Smelling an odor with their nose … | T: Smelling an aroma via the nose…
an3.61:12.7 | S: Tasting a flavor with their tongue … | T: Tasting a flavor via the tongue…
an3.61:12.8 | S: Encountering a touch with their body … | T: Touching a tactile sensation via the body…
an3.61:12.9 | S: Becoming conscious of an idea with their mind, one is preoccupied with an idea that’s a basis for happiness or sadness or equanimity. | T: Cognizing an idea via the intellect, one explores an idea that can act as the basis for happiness, one explores an idea that can act as the basis for unhappiness, one explores an idea that can act as the basis for equanimity.
an3.61:12.10 | S: ‘“These are the eighteen mental preoccupations”: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught …’ | T: ‘“There are these eighteen explorations for the intellect” is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans’:
an3.61:12.11 | S: That’s what I said, and this is why I said it. | T: Thus was it said. And in reference to this was it said.
an3.61:13.1 | S: ‘“These are the four noble truths”: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught …’ | T: “‘“There are these four noble truths” is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans’:
an3.61:13.2 | S: That’s what I said, | T: Thus was it said.
an3.61:13.3 | S: but why did I say it? | T: And in reference to what was it said?
an3.61:13.4 | S: Supported by the six elements, an embryo is conceived. | T: “Sustained by/clinging to the six properties, there is an alighting of an embryo.
an3.61:13.5 | S: When it is conceived, there are name and form. Name and form are requirements for the six sense fields. The six sense fields are requirements for contact. Contact is a requirement for feeling. | T: There being an alighting, there is name-&-form. From name-&-form as a requisite condition come the six sense media. From the six sense media as a requisite condition comes contact. From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling.
an3.61:13.6 | S: It’s for one who feels that I declare: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: To one experiencing feeling I declare, ‘This is stress.’ I declare, ‘This is the origination of stress.’ I declare, ‘This is the cessation of stress.’ I declare, ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
an3.61:14.1 | S: And what is the noble truth of suffering? | T: “And what is the noble truth of stress?
an3.61:14.2 | S: Rebirth is suffering; old age is suffering; death is suffering; sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress are suffering; being coupled with the disliked is suffering; separation from the liked is suffering; not getting what you wish for is suffering. In brief, the five grasping aggregates are suffering. | T: Birth is stress, aging is stress, death is stress; sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair are stress; association with what is not loved is stress, separation from what is loved is stress, not getting what is wanted is stress. In short, the five clinging-aggregates are stress.
an3.61:14.3 | S: This is called the noble truth of suffering. | T: This is called the noble truth of stress.

