an3.61:15.1 | S: And what is the noble truth of the origin of suffering? | T: “And what is the noble truth of the origination of stress?
an3.61:15.2 | S: Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: From ignorance as a requisite condition come fabrications.
an3.61:15.3 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. | T: From fabrications as a requisite condition comes consciousness.
an3.61:15.4 | S: Consciousness is a requirement for name and form. | T: From consciousness as a requisite condition comes name-&-form.
an3.61:15.5 | S: Name and form are requirements for the six sense fields. | T: From name-&-form as a requisite condition come the six sense media.
an3.61:15.6 | S: The six sense fields are requirements for contact. | T: From the six sense media as a requisite condition comes contact.
an3.61:15.7 | S: Contact is a requirement for feeling. | T: From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling.
an3.61:15.8 | S: Feeling is a requirement for craving. | T: From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving.
an3.61:15.9 | S: Craving is a requirement for grasping. | T: From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance.
an3.61:15.10 | S: Grasping is a requirement for continued existence. | T: From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming.
an3.61:15.11 | S: Continued existence is a requirement for rebirth. | T: From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth.
an3.61:15.12 | S: Rebirth is a requirement for old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress to come to be. | T: From birth as a requisite condition, then old age & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
an3.61:15.13 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
an3.61:15.14 | S: This is called the noble truth of the origin of suffering. | T: This is called the noble truth of the origination of stress.
an3.61:16.1 | S: And what is the noble truth of the cessation of suffering? | T: “And what is the noble truth of the cessation of stress?
an3.61:16.2 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: From the remainderless fading & cessation of that very ignorance comes the cessation of fabrications.
an3.61:16.3 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. | T: From the cessation of fabrications comes the cessation of consciousness.
an3.61:16.4 | S: When consciousness ceases, name and form cease. | T: From the cessation of consciousness comes the cessation of name-&-form.
an3.61:16.5 | S: When name and form cease, the six sense fields cease. | T: From the cessation of name-&-form comes the cessation of the six sense media.
an3.61:16.6 | S: When the six sense fields cease, contact ceases. | T: From the cessation of the six sense media comes the cessation of contact.
an3.61:16.7 | S: When contact ceases, feeling ceases. | T: From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of feeling.
an3.61:16.8 | S: When feeling ceases, craving ceases. | T: From the cessation of feeling comes the cessation of craving.
an3.61:16.9 | S: When craving ceases, grasping ceases. | T: From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance.
an3.61:16.10 | S: When grasping ceases, continued existence ceases. | T: From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming.
an3.61:16.11 | S: When continued existence ceases, rebirth ceases. | T: From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth.
an3.61:16.12 | S: When rebirth ceases, old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress cease. | T: From the cessation of birth, then old age & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
an3.61:16.13 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases. | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
an3.61:16.14 | S: This is called the noble truth of the cessation of suffering. | T: “This is called the noble truth of the cessation of stress.
an3.61:17.1 | S: And what is the noble truth of the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering? | T: “And what is the noble truth of the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress?
an3.61:17.2 | S: It is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: Just this noble eightfold path:
an3.61:17.3 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
an3.61:17.4 | S: This is called the noble truth of the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: This is called the noble truth of the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.
an3.61:17.5 | S: ‘“These are the four noble truths”: this is the Dhamma I’ve taught that is irrefutable, uncorrupted, beyond reproach, and is not scorned by sensible ascetics and brahmins.’ | T: “‘“There are these four noble truths” is a Dhamma taught by me that is unrefuted, undefiled, blameless, not faulted by knowledgeable contemplatives & brahmans’:
an3.61:17.6 | S: That’s what I said, and this is why I said it.” | T: Thus was it said. And in reference to this was it said.”
an3.61:17.7 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## thag14.1
thag14.1:1.1 | S: Since I’ve gone forth | T: Since I went forth
thag14.1:1.2 | S: from the lay life to homelessness, | T: from home into homelessness,
thag14.1:1.3 | S: I’ve not been aware of any thought | T: I haven’t known
thag14.1:1.4 | S: that is ignoble and hateful. | T: an ignoble, aversive resolve.
thag14.1:2.1 | S: “May these beings be killed! | T: “May these beings be destroyed,
thag14.1:2.2 | S: May they be slaughtered! May they suffer!”— | T: be slaughtered, fall into pain”—
thag14.1:2.3 | S: I’ve not been aware of any such thought | T: I’ve not known this resolve
thag14.1:2.4 | S: in all this long while. | T: in this long, long time.
thag14.1:3.1 | S: I have been aware of loving-kindness, | T: But I have known goodwill,
thag14.1:3.2 | S: limitless and well-developed; | T: unlimited, fully developed,
thag14.1:3.3 | S: gradually consolidated | T: nurtured step after step,
thag14.1:3.4 | S: as it was taught by the Buddha. | T: as taught by the One Awake:
thag14.1:4.1 | S: I’m friend and comrade to all, | T: to all, a friend; to all, a comrade;
thag14.1:4.2 | S: sympathetic for all beings! | T: for all beings, sympathetic.
thag14.1:4.3 | S: I develop a mind of love, | T: And I develop a mind of goodwill,
thag14.1:4.4 | S: always delighting in harmlessness. | T: delighting in non-malevolence —always.
thag14.1:5.1 | S: The unfaltering, the unshakable: | T: Unvanquished, unshaken,
thag14.1:5.2 | S: I gladden that mind. | T: I gladden the mind.
thag14.1:5.3 | S: I develop a divine meditation, | T: I develop the sublime abiding,
thag14.1:5.4 | S: which reprobates do not cultivate. | T: not frequented by the lowly.
thag14.1:6.1 | S: When in a meditation  free of placing the mind, | T: Attaining no-thinking,
thag14.1:6.2 | S: a disciple of the Buddha | T: the disciple of the Rightly Self-awakened One
thag14.1:6.3 | S: is at that moment blessed | T: is endowed
thag14.1:6.4 | S: with noble silence. | T: with noble silence straightaway.
thag14.1:7.1 | S: As a rocky mountain | T: As a mountain of rock
thag14.1:7.2 | S: is unwavering and well grounded, | T: is unmoving, firmly established,
thag14.1:7.3 | S: so when delusion ends, | T: so a monk, with the ending of delusion,
thag14.1:7.4 | S: a monk, like a mountain, doesn’t tremble. | T: like a mountain, doesn’t quake.
thag14.1:8.1 | S: To the man who has not a blemish, | T: To a person without blemish,
thag14.1:8.2 | S: who is always seeking purity, | T: in constant quest of what’s pure,
thag14.1:8.3 | S: even a hair-tip of evil | T: a hair-tip of evil
thag14.1:8.4 | S: seems as big as a stormcloud. | T: seems a storm cloud.
thag14.1:9.1 | S: As a frontier city | T: As a frontier fortress
thag14.1:9.2 | S: is guarded inside and out, | T: is guarded within & without,
thag14.1:9.3 | S: so you should ward yourselves— | T: you should safeguard yourselves.
thag14.1:9.4 | S: don’t let the moment pass you by. | T: Don’t let the moment pass you by.
thag14.1:10.1 | S: I don’t long for death; | T: I don’t delight in death,
thag14.1:10.2 | S: I don’t long for life; | T: don’t delight in living.
thag14.1:10.3 | S: I await my time, | T: I await my time
thag14.1:10.4 | S: like a worker waiting for their wages. | T: like a worker his wage.
thag14.1:11.1 | S: I don’t long for death; | T: I don’t delight in death,
thag14.1:11.2 | S: I don’t long for life; | T: don’t delight in living.
thag14.1:11.3 | S: I await my time, | T: I await my time,
thag14.1:11.4 | S: aware and mindful. | T: mindful, alert.
thag14.1:12.1 | S: I’ve served the Teacher | T: The Teacher has been served by me;
thag14.1:12.2 | S: and fulfilled the Buddha’s instructions. | T: the Awakened One’s bidding, done;
thag14.1:12.3 | S: The heavy burden is laid down, | T: the heavy load, laid down;
thag14.1:12.4 | S: the leash to existence is eradicated. | T: the guide to becoming, uprooted.
thag14.1:13.1 | S: I’ve attained the goal | T: And the goal for which I went forth
thag14.1:13.2 | S: for the sake of which I went forth | T: (none)
thag14.1:13.3 | S: from the lay life to homelessness— | T: from home life into homelessness I’ve reached:
thag14.1:13.4 | S: the ending of all fetters. | T: the end of all fetters.
thag14.1:14.1 | S: Persist with diligence: | T: Attain consummation through heedfulness:
thag14.1:14.2 | S: this is my instruction. | T: That is my message.
thag14.1:14.3 | S: Come, I’ll be fully quenched— | T: So then, I’m about to unbind.
thag14.1:14.4 | S: I’m liberated in every way. | T: I’m released everywhere.
thag14.1:14.5 | P: … Khadiravaniyarevato thero …. | T: (none)

## sn22.101
sn22.101:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī. There the Blessed One said,
sn22.101:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I say that the ending of defilements is for one who knows and sees, not for one who does not know or see. | T: “I tell you, monks: It is for one who knows & sees that there is the ending of effluents.
sn22.101:1.3 | S: For one who knows and sees what? | T: For one who knows & sees what is there the ending of effluents?
sn22.101:1.4 | S: ‘Such is form, such is the origin of form, such is the disappearance of form. | T: ‘Such is form, such its origination, such its passing away.
sn22.101:1.5 | S: Such is feeling … | T: Such is feeling.…
sn22.101:1.6 | S: Such is perception … | T: Such is perception.…
sn22.101:1.7 | S: Such are choices … | T: Such are fabrications.…
sn22.101:1.8 | S: Such is consciousness, such is the origin of consciousness, such is the disappearance of consciousness.’ | T: Such is consciousness, such its origination, such its passing away.’
sn22.101:1.9 | S: The ending of the defilements is for one who knows and sees this. | T: It is for one who knows & sees in this way that there is the ending of effluents.
sn22.101:2.1 | S: When a mendicant is not committed to development, they might wish: | T: “Even though this wish may occur to a monk who dwells without devoting himself to development—
sn22.101:2.2 | S: ‘If only my mind were freed from the defilements by not grasping!’ Even so, their mind is not freed from defilements by not grasping. | T: ‘O that my mind might be released from effluents through lack of clinging!’—still his mind is not released from effluents through lack of clinging.
sn22.101:2.3 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn22.101:2.4 | S: You should say: ‘It’s because they’re undeveloped.’ | T: From lack of developing, it should be said.
sn22.101:2.5 | S: Undeveloped in what? | T: Lack of developing what?
sn22.101:2.6 | S: Undeveloped in the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, the four right efforts, the four bases of psychic power, the five faculties, the five powers, the seven awakening factors, and the noble eightfold path. | T: The four establishing of mindfulness, the four right exertions, the four bases of power, the five faculties, the five strengths, the seven factors for awakening, the noble eightfold path.
sn22.101:3.1 | S: Suppose there was a chicken with eight or ten or twelve eggs. | T: “Suppose a hen has eight, ten, or twelve eggs:
sn22.101:3.2 | S: But she had not properly sat on them to keep them warm and incubated. | T: If she doesn’t cover them rightly, warm them rightly, or incubate them rightly,
sn22.101:3.3 | S: That chicken might wish: | T: then even though this wish may occur to her—
sn22.101:3.4 | S: ‘If only my chicks could break out of the eggshell with their claws and beak and hatch safely!’ | T: ‘O that my chicks might break through the egg shells with their spiked claws or beaks and hatch out safely!’—
sn22.101:3.5 | S: But they can’t break out and hatch safely. | T: still it is not possible that the chicks will break through the egg shells with their spiked claws or beaks and hatch out safely.
sn22.101:3.6 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn22.101:3.7 | S: Because that chicken with eight or ten or twelve eggs | T: Because the hen
sn22.101:3.8 | S: has not properly sat on them to keep them warm and incubated. | T: has not covered them rightly, warmed them rightly, or incubated them rightly.
sn22.101:3.9 | S: In the same way, when a mendicant is not committed to development, they might wish: | T: In the same way, even though this wish may occur to a monk who dwells without devoting himself to development—
sn22.101:3.10 | S: ‘If only my mind were freed from the defilements by not grasping!’ Even so, their mind is not freed from defilements by not grasping. | T: ‘O that my mind might be released from effluents through lack of clinging!’—still his mind is not released from effluents through lack of clinging.
sn22.101:3.11 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn22.101:3.12 | S: You should say: ‘It’s because they’re undeveloped.’ | T: From lack of developing, it should be said.
sn22.101:3.13 | S: Undeveloped in what? | T: Lack of developing what?
sn22.101:3.14 | S: Undeveloped in the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, the four right efforts, the four bases of psychic power, the five faculties, the five powers, the seven awakening factors, and the noble eightfold path. | T: The four establishing of mindfulness, the four right exertions, the four bases of power, the five faculties, the five strengths, the seven factors for awakening, the noble eightfold path.
sn22.101:4.1 | S: When a mendicant is committed to development, they might not wish: | T: “Even though this wish may not occur to a monk who dwells devoting himself to development—
sn22.101:4.2 | S: ‘If only my mind were freed from the defilements by not grasping!’ Even so, their mind is freed from defilements by not grasping. | T: ‘O that my mind might be released from effluents through lack of clinging!’—still his mind is released from effluents through lack of clinging.
sn22.101:4.3 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn22.101:4.4 | S: You should say: ‘It’s because they are developed.’ | T: From developing, it should be said.
sn22.101:4.5 | S: Developed in what? | T: Developing what?
sn22.101:4.6 | S: Developed in the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, the four right efforts, the four bases of psychic power, the five faculties, the five powers, the seven awakening factors, and the noble eightfold path. | T: The four establishing of mindfulness, the four right exertions, the four bases of power, the five faculties, the five strengths, the seven factors for awakening, the noble eightfold path.
sn22.101:5.1 | S: Suppose there was a chicken with eight or ten or twelve eggs. | T: “Suppose a hen has eight, ten, or twelve eggs
sn22.101:5.2 | S: And she properly sat on them to keep them warm and incubated. | T: that she covers rightly, warms rightly, & incubates rightly:
sn22.101:5.3 | S: That chicken might not wish: | T: Even though this wish may not occur to her—
sn22.101:5.4 | S: ‘If only my chicks could break out of the eggshell with their claws and beak and hatch safely!’ | T: ‘O that my chicks might break through the egg shells with their spiked claws or beaks and hatch out safely!’—
sn22.101:5.5 | S: But still they can break out and hatch safely. | T: still it is possible that the chicks will break through the egg shells with their spiked claws or beaks and hatch out safely.
sn22.101:5.6 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn22.101:5.7 | S: Because that chicken with eight or ten or twelve eggs | T: Because the hen
sn22.101:5.8 | S: properly sat on them to keep them warm and incubated. | T: has covered them, warmed them, & incubated them rightly.
sn22.101:5.9 | S: In the same way, when a mendicant is committed to development, they might not wish: | T: In the same way, even though this wish may not occur to a monk who dwells devoting himself to development—
sn22.101:5.10 | S: ‘If only my mind were freed from the defilements by not grasping!’ Even so, their mind is freed from defilements by not grasping. | T: ‘O that my mind might be released from effluents through lack of clinging!’—still his mind is released from effluents through lack of clinging.
sn22.101:5.11 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn22.101:5.12 | S: You should say: ‘It’s because they are developed.’ | T: From developing, it should be said.
sn22.101:5.13 | S: Developed in what? | T: Developing what?
sn22.101:5.14 | S: Developed in the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, the four right efforts, the four bases of psychic power, the five faculties, the five powers, the seven awakening factors, and the noble eightfold path. | T: The four establishing of mindfulness, the four right exertions, the four bases of power, the five faculties, the five strengths, the seven factors for awakening, the noble eightfold path.
sn22.101:6.1 | S: Suppose a mason or their apprentice sees the marks of his fingers and thumb on the handle of his adze. | T: “Just as when a carpenter or carpenter’s apprentice sees the marks of his fingers or thumb on the handle of his adze but does not know,
sn22.101:6.2 | S: They don’t know | T: (none)
sn22.101:6.3 | S: how much of the handle was worn away today, how much yesterday, and how much previously. | T: ‘Today my adze handle wore down this much, or yesterday it wore down that much, or the day before yesterday it wore down this much,’
sn22.101:6.4 | S: They just know what has been worn away. | T: still he knows it is worn through when it is worn through.
sn22.101:6.5 | S: In the same way, when a mendicant is committed to development, they don’t know | T: In the same way, when a monk dwells devoting himself to development, he does not know,
sn22.101:6.6 | S: how much of the defilements were worn away today, how much yesterday, and how much previously. They just know what has been worn away. | T: ‘Today my effluents wore down this much, or yesterday they wore down that much, or the day before yesterday they wore down this much,’ still he knows they are worn through when they are worn through.
sn22.101:6.7 | S: Suppose there was a sea-faring ship bound together with ropes. For six months they deteriorated in the water. Then in the cold season it was hauled up on dry land, where the ropes were weathered by wind and sun. When the monsoon clouds soaked it with rain, the ropes would readily collapse and rot away. | T: “Just as when an ocean-going ship, rigged with masts & stays, after six months on the water, is left on shore for the winter: Its stays, weathered by the heat & wind, moistened by the clouds of the rainy season, easily wither & rot away.
sn22.101:6.8 | S: In the same way, when a mendicant is committed to development their fetters readily collapse and rot away.” | T: In the same way, when a monk dwells devoting himself to development, his fetters easily wither & rot away.”
sn22.101:6.9 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)


